3. OpticalDNA
3.4. Visual Encoder and Document Decoder
Document decoder and <image> injection. We employ an autoregressive document decoder G ψ based on DeepSeek-3B (Mixture-of-Experts, 570M activated parameters) from DeepSeek-OCR for prompt-conditioned genomic reasoning. Each instance is represented as a twoturn multimodal conversation C = [ m ( u ) , m ( a ) ] . The user message contains a single reserved <image> placeholder, whose images fi eld carries an ordered list of P page images { page p } P -1 p =0 . To explicitly specify the number of images bound to the placeholder, we append a meta line \ nNUM IMAGES= P . immediately after <image> in the prompt. Conditioned on the fused visual tokens Z and a task prompt q ∈ Q , the decoder autoregressively generates the task-dependent output sequence ˆ Y = (ˆ y 1 , . . . , ˆ y T ) :
<!-- formula-not-decoded -->
where V denotes the decoder vocabulary.

--- CHUNK 14 ---
3. OpticalDNA
3.5. Training Objectives and Optimization
Prompt-conditioned generative pretraining. We pretrain OpticalDNA with the unified OCR-style prompting protocol (Section 3.3). Given a prompt q ∈ Q and fused visual tokens Z = E θ ( { page p } P -1 p =0 ) , we optimize the standard teacher-forced autoregressive objective on the assistant response tokens y = ( y 1 , . . . , y T ) :
<!-- formula-not-decoded -->
where the loss is computed only over the assistant span.
Task balancing and instance randomization. To balance the six prompt families and improve robustness, we sample the task index t ∼ Cat( π ) with π = ( π 1 , . . . , π 6 ) for T1-T6. To improve robustness for ROI-centric supervision, we optionally apply tail truncation to tasks that rely on page-level region items (T2/T3/T5), by deleting a suffix of line-/box-level supervision items with a base rate ρ base and an additional randomized rate capped by ρ max . For line-/block-based supervision, we randomize the span by sampling n samp ∼ Uniform { n min samp , . . . , n max samp } , optionally sampling source lines without replacement (Unique lines). For subsequence localization (T5), we sample the query length ℓ ∼ Uniform { ℓ min , . . . , ℓ max } and control whether overlapping matches are allowed.
Optimization and trainable components. We freeze the DeepSeek-OCR visual front-end (SAM-Conv-CLIP-L). The document decoder G ψ is fine-tuned with LoRA, the multi-page fusion module F θ is trained with full-parameter updating, and the projector Π θ is tuned either with LoRA or full-parameter updating depending on the setting (See Section 4.1). This design enables efficient adaptation while preserving the stability of large pretrained backbones.

--- CHUNK 15 ---
4. Experiments
4.1. Experimental Settings
Pre-training. All pretraining experiments are conducted on 8 × H100 GPUs. For fair comparison with prior work (Nguyen et al., 2023a; Duan et al., 2025), we pretrain OpticalDNA on HG38 (Schneider et al., 2017) using the same sequence sampling protocol as JanusDNA (Duan et al., 2025).


